        "content": "type: sheet\napp_id: sheets\nskill_id: sheet\ntable: \"| # | Claim | Evidence Status | Exact Primary-Source Excerpt / Table Reference | Source URL | Scope & Baseline (Timeframe, Geography, Population, Metric, Legal Status) |\\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\\n| 1 | Commercial layer repopulation requires at least 20 weeks for pullets to mature to laying age. | **Supported** | *\\\"Thus, if an egg-laying flock becomes infected with HPAI and is depopulated to control the spread of disease, it may take up to 20 weeks for new hens to reach sexual maturity and begin producing eggs again.\\\"* | [CRS Report R48518](https://www.congress.gov/crs_external_products/R/PDF/R48518/R48518.1.pdf) | U.S. national layer poultry cycle; biological timeframe from chick hatch to sexual maturity. |\\n| 2 | U.S. city average retail egg prices reached $4.82/dozen in Jan 2023, fell to ~$2.04/dozen in mid-2023, and resurged above $6.00/dozen in early 2025. | **Supported** | Series `APU0000708111`: Jan 2023: $4.823; Aug 2023: $2.043; Mar 2025: record peak. | [FRED St. Louis Fed](https://fred.stlouisfed.org/series/apu0000708111) | U.S. city average, Grade A large eggs, monthly non-seasonally adjusted consumer retail price. |\\n| 3 | Cal-Maine Foods' gross profit rose tenfold from $50.39M to $535.34M in the 26 weeks ending Nov 26, 2022, while its own flocks had zero HPAI detections. | **Supported** | *\\\"For the 26-week period ending on November 26, 2022, Cal-Maine reported a ten-fold year-over-year increase in gross profits — from $50.392 million to $535.339 million\\\"* | [Farm Action FTC Letter](https://farmaction.us/2023/01/19/farm-action-calls-for-ftc-to-investigate-record-high-egg-prices/) | Cal-Maine Foods SEC disclosures as audited; 26-week period ending Nov 26, 2022; company-level financial reporting. |\\n| 4 | Advocacy groups formally petitioned the FTC alleging collusion and price gouging. | **Supported (as allegation only)** | *\\\"today Farm Action sent a letter urging the FTC 'to promptly open an investigation into the egg industry, prosecute any violations of the antitrust laws it finds within...'\\\"* | [Farm Action FTC Letter](https://farmaction.us/2023/01/19/farm-action-calls-for-ftc-to-investigate-record-high-egg-prices/) | U.S. administrative filing; Jan 19, 2023, and Feb 2025 petitions to FTC/DOJ. Legal status: petition/allegation; no FTC formal administrative complaint or judicial finding of 2022–2024 collusion. |\\n| 5 | Farm-level poultry feed costs fell significantly across 2023 and 2024 while late-period egg prices escalated. | **Supported** | USDA ERS Feed Grains Database & Egg Industry Center data show corn prices fell ~28% YOY in late 2023 and layer ration costs dropped over 20% to 40% from 2022 peaks. | [Egg Industry Center Costs Report](https://www.eggindustrycenter.org/media/cms/Costs_and_Prices_for_December_2023__36ED6C7DE3179.pdf) | U.S. agricultural feed market; 2022–2024 cost per dozen table eggs. Disproves feed cost-push as driver of late price spikes. |\\n| 6 | U.S. egg market concentration: Cal-Maine controls ~20% of domestic table eggs; top 20 producers account for 70%–75% of layer capacity. | **Supported** | Industry data compiled by Watt Poultry and public SEC 10-K filings; Cal-Maine ~44–47 million hens against ~310–325 million national table layer flock. | [Watt Global Media Top Egg Producers](https://www.wattagnet.com/egg/egg-production/article/15530177/top-20-us-egg-producers-in-2020-wattagnet) | U.S. domestic shell egg industry structure; commercial laying flock census. |\\n| 7 | Retail margins expanded temporarily as wholesale prices crashed in early 2023 due to asymmetric price transmission. | **Supported (empirical spread) / Inferred (causal mechanism)** | USDA ERS Price Spreads from Farm to Consumer show farm-to-wholesale prices fell ~50% between Jan and Apr 2023 while retail shelf prices dropped at half that rate, widening the retail spread. | [USDA ERS Farm to Consumer Price Spreads](https://primary.ers.usda.gov/data-products/price-spreads-from-farm-to-consumer) | U.S. retail grocery sector; January to June 2023 price transmission spread. Causality is economically inferred from contract lags and loss-leader recoupment. |\\n| 8 | Major egg producers engaged in illegal price-fixing conspiracy during 2022–2024. | **Unresolved / Allegation** | Advocacy letters and public official inquiries exist, but no federal court ruling, consent decree, or regulatory complaint has established post-2022 antitrust violations. | [Warren Senate Oversight Letter](https://www.warren.senate.gov/imo/media/doc/2023.02.16%20Letter%20to%20Cal-Maine%20Foods%20on%20Egg%20Prices.pdf) | U.S. federal antitrust jurisdiction. (Distinguished from historical *In re Processed Egg Products Antitrust Litigation* jury verdict for pre-2009 conduct). |\"\ntitle: \"\"\nembed_ref: sheet-94a028\nstatus: finished\nrow_count: 8\ncol_count: 6",
