                "content": "type: search_result\ntitle: CDFA - Animal Health - Avian Influenza\nurl: \"https://www.cdfa.ca.gov/ahfss/Animal_Health/Avian_Influenza.html\"\ndescription: \"This nationwide outbreak of HPAI ... HPAI virus spreads easily between birds and is lethal to poultry, so infected flocks are quarantined and euthanized, followed by cleaning and disinfection of the barns....\"\nage: null\npage_age: null\nlanguage: en\nfamily_friendly: true\nprofile_name: CA\nmeta_url_favicon: \"https://imgs.search.brave.com/Pvs-r8TeJBGCdm10i-cvO5YiAxGAyXMm7_4ZRoyx6cs/rs:fit:32:32:1:0/g:ce/aHR0cDovL2Zhdmlj/b25zLnNlYXJjaC5i/cmF2ZS5jb20vaWNv/bnMvMGRkOTdkY2I2/NGVmNTI2NzZlMmM5/OGM1ZjIzNTNmMTEy/NTM5Zjc0MGY5M2Zj/MGQ1OTgzN2VhZTQ2/NjVlNzkyYy93d3cu/Y2RmYS5jYS5nb3Yv\"\nthumbnail: null\nextra_snippets: \"This nationwide outbreak of HPAI began in 2022 and all US detections in poultry can be found here: 2022–2024 Detections of Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (usda.gov). Note that the website is updated at noon Eastern Standard Time. This HPAI virus spreads easily between birds and is lethal to poultry, so infected flocks are quarantined and euthanized, followed by cleaning and disinfection of the barns.|In contrast, HPAI causes a severe illness with a high mortality rate among infected birds. Because some LPAI strains have the potential to genetically change into HPAI, authorities will depopulate flocks infected with those strains of LPAI before they have the potential to change to HPAI and cause severe mortality.|Due to the investigator’s assessment, no movement restrictions will be placed in El Dorado County however, movement restrictions are still place in Sonoma County. This nationwide outbreak of HPAI began in 2022 and all US detections in poultry can be found here: 2022–2024 Detections of Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (usda.gov)2022–2024 Detections of Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (usda.gov).|This can be due to no lab submissions from those counties. We strongly urge all poultry owners in California to protect their flocks by increasing their biosecurity practices. Recommended biosecurity practices can be found on the 2022–2024 Detections of Highly Pathogenic Avian Influenza (usda.gov) websites.\"\nhash: 1cc2f26ca83f054d\nembed_ref: cdfa.ca.gov-MsX\napp_id: web\nskill_id: search",
