                "content": "type: sheet\napp_id: sheets\nskill_id: sheet\ntable: \"| Claim # | Claim Description | Evidence Status | Exact Primary-Source URL | Scope & Limitations | Exact Excerpt / Primary-Table Metric |\\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\\n| **1** | U.S. city average retail egg prices rose sharply to exceed $4.00/dozen in late 2022, rising ~37% year-over-year. | Supported | `https://retailwire.com/discussion/should-grocers-be-pricing-eggs-at-a-loss/` | December 2022; U.S. urban city average; Grade A large eggs; BLS monthly retail price. | *\\\"A dozen large grade A eggs cost an average of $4.15 in the U.S. in December — a nearly 37% increase from the year before — according to data from the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics.\\\"* |\\n| **2** | Spot wholesale benchmark prices for Midwest large eggs surged during peak disruption periods to $7.27/dozen, at times exceeding retail shelf prices. | Supported | `https://retailwire.com/discussion/should-grocers-be-pricing-eggs-at-a-loss/` | Peak disruption months (late 2022 / late 2024); Midwest regional wholesale benchmark; Grade A large eggs. | *\\\"Wholesale prices have risen even higher, with Midwest large eggs costing $7.27 per dozen, as reported by the global commodities tracker Expana.\\\"* |\\n| **3** | U.S. retail egg prices moderated from peak levels but remained volatile, averaging $3.204 per dozen by August 2024. | Supported | `https://www.scribd.com/document/851555175/Average-Price-Eggs-Grade-A-Large-Cost-per-Dozen-in-U-S-City-Average-APU0000708111-FRED-St-Louis-Fed` | August 2024; U.S. urban city average; Grade A large eggs; monthly non-seasonally adjusted average price. | *\\\"As of August 2024, the average price for a dozen Grade A large eggs in the U.S. is $3.204, based on data collected by the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics.\\\"* |\\n| **4** | Downstream prices frequently exhibit asymmetric transmission relative to upstream costs (\\\"rockets and feathers\\\"). | Supported | `https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asymmetric_price_transmission` | Conceptual and empirical economic pricing literature; downstream vs. upstream markets. | *\\\"The concept is known colloquially as 'rocket and feather pricing' or 'rockets and feathers', an analogy which describes the price rising rapidly like a rocket, but falling slowly like a feather.\\\"* |\\n| **5** | Empirical economic literature identifies asymmetric price adjustment across more than two-thirds of examined product categories. | Supported | `https://www.ebc.com/forex/what-is-the-rockets-and-feathers-effect` | Cross-commodity analysis (Sam Peltzman 2000 study); 242 consumer/producer goods markets; U.S. data. | *\\\"Economist Sam Peltzman’s 2000 study examined 242 consumer and producer goods markets and found asymmetric price adjustments in more than two-thirds of them.\\\"* |\\n| **6** | The [Economic Research Service](wiki:Economic_Research_Service) (ERS) of the USDA formally calculates price spreads from farm to consumer across meat, poultry, and egg categories. | Supported | `https://primary.ers.usda.gov/data-products/price-spreads-from-farm-to-consumer` | Ongoing U.S. national series; farm-to-wholesale-to-retail price spreads; USDA agricultural reporting. | *\\\"ERS compares the prices paid by consumers for food with the prices received by farmers for their corresponding commodities. These comparisons are reported for a variety of foods sold in retail food stores.\\\"* |\\n| **7** | Inelastic short-run demand creates an asymmetric pricing environment where upward cost shocks are passed on quickly with little volume loss. | Supported | `https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S240585132600019X` | Microeconomic commodity pricing dynamics; threshold pricing analysis; short-run consumer elasticity. | *\\\"demand is highly inelastic in the short run, allowing retailers to pass through cost increases without significant demand loss while facing little pressure to cut prices when costs fall\\\"* |\\n| **8** | Unlawful retailer price-fixing or collusive market manipulation caused the widening of post-peak retail egg margins. | Unresolved | `https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/abs/pii/S0167268118301112` | 2022–2024 U.S. supermarket sector; legal adjudications and antitrust filings. | *\\\"The first potential reason that springs to mind for why prices adjust asymmetrically to cost shocks is market power and (tacit) collusion\\\"* (Theoretical hypothesis; unconfirmed by retail antitrust judgments). |\"\ntitle: \"\"\nembed_ref: sheet-2c2812\nstatus: finished\nrow_count: 8\ncol_count: 6",
