                "embed_id": "7d08467d-9a87-4b8e-ad98-4c7ce2a97091",
                "type": "website",
                "content": "type: search_result\ntitle: \"'Rockets and feathers': Economists say grocery prices shoot up like the first, fall like the second | Fortune\"\nurl: \"https://fortune.com/2026/07/25/grocery-prices-rockets-feathers-inflation/\"\ndescription: \"That’s been frustrating for Americans hit with the sharpest increase in grocery prices in 50 years. While food price inflation peaked in 2022, when prices jumped 11.4%, prices have not reversed course. An acceleration in flation after the U.S.\"\nage: \"July 25, 2026\"\npage_age: \"July 25, 2026\"\nlanguage: en\nfamily_friendly: true\nprofile_name: Fortune\nmeta_url_favicon: \"https://imgs.search.brave.com/qpaf191ageaCLkoiVd7yZgjgunQBvqCCAoYKwHLz71U/rs:fit:32:32:1:0/g:ce/aHR0cDovL2Zhdmlj/b25zLnNlYXJjaC5i/cmF2ZS5jb20vaWNv/bnMvZDc2NDViM2Vj/Mjk2NzE1OGRlYTYy/MWUwYzg4NGI1NGE2/NDg0NzdhNzE5ZjM2/OGE3MmIwZTAyZDE2/NzBkNWU2Yi9mb3J0/dW5lLmNvbS8\"\nthumbnail_src: \"https://imgs.search.brave.com/7gByJeUQYwR8oPeE2OLt1kqxgH35AG9mnypXyTBOHfg/rs:fit:200:200:1:0/g:ce/aHR0cHM6Ly9mb3J0/dW5lLmNvbS9pbWct/YXNzZXRzL3dwLWNv/bnRlbnQvdXBsb2Fk/cy8yMDI2LzA3L0FQ/MjYyMDE4NDA1NDAx/NDQtZTE3ODQ5ODcz/MDA2MDQuanBn\"\nthumbnail_original: \"https://fortune.com/img-assets/wp-content/uploads/2026/07/AP26201840540144-e1784987300604.jpg\"\nextra_snippets: \"Stores and food companies also want to maximize their profits and were reluctant to give up some of the sales gains they saw after the pandemic, Bernstein said. PepsiCo hiked its prices by double-digit percentages for eight straight quarters in 2022 and 2023, for example, citing higher ingredient and packaging costs.|Prices for food at home are expected to rise 2.7% across the country this year, the U.S. Department of Agriculture reported Friday. That’s higher than the grocery inflation shoppers saw in 2024 and 2025 but close to the historical average of 2.6%.|That’s been frustrating for Americans hit with the sharpest increase in grocery prices in 50 years. While food price inflation peaked in 2022, when prices jumped 11.4%, prices have not reversed course. An acceleration in flation after the U.S.\"\nhash: df0b47f62a2edb6d\nembed_ref: fortune.com-GIH\napp_id: web\nskill_id: search",
